        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": {
            "en": "Source A §20 gives ARO = 2 for two events per year, 0.2 for an average of one in five years, and 0.5 for an average of one in two years. Interpret these as estimated frequencies over time, not scheduled events that must occur every fifth or second year. ARO can exceed 1, unlike a probability constrained to 0–1. ARO 0.2 is not 0.2%, and frequency 0.2 per year does not by itself establish an exact 20% probability of at least one event in a year without an additional event-distribution model. Use the same event definition and time basis throughout. For example, two events per month correspond to an annual frequency of 24 if that rate is assumed to continue; uncertainty in that assumption should be reported.",
